# Treatment Philosophy and Guidelines for the Battlefield Commemorative Area (Auwaerter, OCLP, 2025): Historical Overview, PDF pp. 33-45.
# Dated commemorative features: every one of them was placed AFTER July 1863 (state dates start with '!'): never drawn in the 1863 scene.
# Quotes are typed in the corrected spelling ('=' form): the text layer lost the i of fi ligatures; the builder checks every letter against the page.
@ NPS-TPG-COMMEM
33 | later | Origins of the Commemorative Area: GBMA 1864, monuments by 1888 | - | !1864-1888 | stated | The Battlefield Commemorative Area traces to the founding of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association in 1864 and to the hundreds of monuments added around the twenty-fifth anniversary in 1888 | =The origins of the Battlefield Commemorative Area trace back to the founding of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association in 1864 and the addition of hundreds of monuments around the time of the twenty-fifth anniversary of the battle in 1888.
33 | later | Gettysburg National Park Commission 1893-1922 | - | !1893-1922 | stated | Federal management began in 1893 with the veteran-led Gettysburg National Park Commission, which administered the park from its establishment in 1895 through 1922 | =Federal involvement in the management of the battlefield park began in 1893 during the waning years of the Memorial Association with Congress’s creation of the veteran-led Gettysburg National Park Commission, which administered Gettysburg National Military Park from its establishment in 1895 through 1922.
33 | monuments | Eternal Light Peace Memorial 1938 | - | !1938 | stated | The last veteran-associated commemorative addition was the Eternal Light Peace Memorial, dedicated in 1938 | =The last veteran-associated commemorative addition was the Eternal Light Peace Memorial dedicated in 1938.
35 | fences | Memorial Association wire fencing chosen to differ from the vernacular rail fences of 1863 | - | 1863 (statement about the 1863 fences); wire fencing 1880s | stated | The Memorial Association used Brinkerhoff wire fencing on park boundaries across open fields because it was distinguishable from the vernacular rail fencing that characterized the 1863 battlefield landscape | =Installation of Brinkerhoff wire fencing (a type of turned wire) along the boundaries of park property that crossed open fields. The Memorial Association selected this fencing because it was distinguishable from vernacular rail fencing that characterized the 1863 battlefield landscape, while also being compatible through its inconspicuous appearance
35 | earthworks | Memorial Association rebuilt earthworks and kept battle-era woodlots and fields | E-12 | !1880s (rebuilding of earthworks) | stated | Rebuilding of earthworks and preservation of battle-era woodlots and fields are among the developments the Memorial Association had completed or overseen by the early 1890s | =Rebuilding of earthworks and preservation of battle-era woodlots and fields. | =By the early 1890s, the Memorial Association had completed, or overseen, the following developments in the park:
35 | fences | Ornamental iron fences at East Cemetery Hill (about 1889) and the Copse of Trees (1886) | P-01 | !1886-1889 | stated | Ornamental iron fences were installed at East Cemetery Hill (Lafayette Square Fence, about 1889) and around the Copse of Trees at the High Water Mark (1886) | =Installation of ornamental iron fences to denote sacred ground at East Cemetery Hill (Lafayette Square Fence, installed about 1889), and around the Copse of Trees at the High Water Mark (1886).
36 | monuments | About 339 monuments and one statue by 1895; High Water Mark memorial 1892 | - | !1888-1895 | stated | By 1895 there were about 339 monuments and one statue on Memorial Association property plus the High Water Mark of the Rebellion memorial dedicated in 1892 | =Many of these regimental monuments were installed or in the works by the twenty-fifth anniversary of the battle in 1888, and by 1895, there were approximately 339 monuments and one statue on Memorial Association property, plus the High Water Mark of the Rebellion memorial along Hancock Avenue dedicated in 1892.
36 | later | First federal funds to mark positions, 1887 and 1893 | - | !1887-1893 | stated | In 1887 Congress appropriated the first funds to mark the positions of US Regulars and in 1893 funds to mark Confederate positions and to buy land and open avenues | =In 1887, Congress appropriated the first funds to the War Department to mark positions of U.S. Regulars, and in 1893, Congress appropriated funds to mark C.S. positions and for acquiring land and opening avenues along those lines.
37 | later | West Confederate Avenue, first federally funded project, by 1894 | - | !1894 | stated | By 1894 work had begun on West Confederate Avenue, the first federally funded project, and on marking US Regulars artillery positions | =By 1894, work had begun on the first federally-funded project, the construction of West Confederate Avenue, and on marking U.S. Regulars artillery positions.
26 | earthworks | East Cemetery Hill lunettes rebuilt in the 1880s; Rodes's breastworks rebuilt about 1896 in stone | E-12,E-18 | 1880s-1896 rebuilt (not an 1863 profile) | stated | East Cemetery Hill lunettes were rebuilt with added earth and turf in the 1880s, and Rodes's breastworks on Seminary Avenue were rebuilt about 1896 with a stone wall in place of the original parapet wall of stacked rails that had disappeared after the battle | =Examples include artillery lunettes on East Cemetery Hill rebuilt with added earth and turf in the 1880s, and Rodes’s CS Division breastworks along Seminary Avenue rebuilt around 1896 with a stone wall in place of the original parapet wall of stacked rails that had disappeared after the battle
40 | later | Park Commission improvements 1895-1905 | - | !1895-1905 | stated | Many of the Park Commission's improvements to the commemorative area were completed between 1895 and 1905 | =Many of the commission’s improvements to the commemorative area of the battlefield were completed between 1895 and 1905.
40 | later | Monuments, statues, markers and avenues after Cope's death in 1927 | - | !about 1927-1933 | hedged | By the time of the changes made after E. B. Cope's death in 1927 the national military park contained approximately 384 regimental monuments, 19 statues and 350 military markers (tablets), and about twenty-four miles of military avenues | =By this time, the national military park contained approximately 384 regimental monuments, 19 statues, and 350 military markers (tablets), and about twenty-four miles of military avenues. | =Cope serving until his death in 1927.
42 | later | Military avenues: 24 miles by 1912, 41 avenues by 1920 | - | !1912-1920 | stated | By 1912 the park's military avenues extended across twenty-four miles, and by 1920 several additional short avenues brought the total to forty-one military avenues | =By 1912, the park’s military avenues extended across twenty-four miles, and by 1920, the mileage was extended with the construction of several additional short avenues, bringing the total to forty-one military avenues.
42 | later | Shell stones: 239 by 1899 | - | !1899 | stated | By 1899 there were 239 shell stones across the park | =By 1899, there were 239 shell stones across the park.
43 | later | Four steel observation towers completed November 1895 | - | !1895 | stated | Construction of the four steel observation towers (planned for Oak Ridge, Big Round Top, West Confederate Avenue and Culp's Hill) was completed in November 1895 | =Production was contracted to the Variety Iron Works of Cleveland, Ohio, and construction on all four was completed in November 1895
43 | later | Fifth observation tower in Ziegler's Grove, 1896 | - | !1896 | stated | A fifth tower, in Ziegler's Grove, was built the year after the four completed in November 1895 | =A fifth tower, in Ziegler’s Grove, was built the following year. | =Production was contracted to the Variety Iron Works of Cleveland, Ohio, and construction on all four was completed in November 1895
44 | fences | Pipe-rail avenue fence of 1897 where no fence stood in 1863 | - | !1897 (avenue fence); 1863 (no fences along the avenues) | stated | The pipe-rail avenue fence (design finalized in 1897) was a contemporary design for use where no fences existed along the avenues in 1863, so that it would be distinguishable from the wood post-and-rail fences that characterized the vernacular battlefield | =The new avenue fences were a contemporary design for use where no fences existed along the avenues in 1863, so that they would be distinguishable—clearly different from the wood post-and-rail fences that characterized the vernacular battlefield. | =The selected proposal, which was finalized in 1897
44 | fences | Park Commission used 1863-style wood fences along 1863 public roads | - | 1895-1905 (park commission fences along roads that existed in 1863) | stated | The Park Commission used 1863-style wood fences outside the avenue system, notably along public roads that existed in 1863 such as Emmitsburg Road, Wheatfield Road and Chambersburg Pike | =It used 1863-style wood fences outside of the military avenue system, notably along public roads that existed in 1863, such as Emmitsburg Road, Wheatfield Road, and Chambersburg Pike.
